  "text": "Rain is a fundamental component of Earth\u2019s hydrological system, formed through a continuous process known as the water cycle. To understand how rain forms, we must first look at the movement of water from the oceans, lakes, and rivers. When the sun heats the surface of these bodies of water, liquid water evaporates and turns into water vapor. This invisible gas rises into the atmosphere because it is lighter than the surrounding air. As the vapor ascends, it enters cooler regions of the atmosphere. This drop in temperature causes the water vapor to condense, changing back into tiny liquid droplets or ice crystals. These particles cluster around microscopic dust or pollen grains, forming clouds.\n\nAs more water vapor condenses, the droplets within the cloud grow larger and heavier. Eventually, gravity pulls these droplets down to the earth as precipitation. If the air temperature is above freezing, this precipitation falls as rain. If it is below freezing, it may fall as snow or sleet. This process is not a one-time event but a perpetual loop that recycles the planet\u2019s water supply.\n\nThe water cycle is indispensable for sustaining life, particularly for plants and river systems. For plants, rain is the primary source of fresh water. When rain falls, it penetrates the soil",
